         <
          C1: synEMPTY_PROSENT IN VERBPPROPrec2.synvpefs AND
              synEMPTY_PROSENT IN VERBrec1.synvps AND
              PROSENTVARrec1.so = false AND
              rel1 = indobjrel AND
              rel2 = complrel 
          A1: VERBPPROPrec1 := VERBPPROPrec2;
              VERBPPROPrec1.synvpefs := []
         >

   SUBRULE (* 14. x1 allow x2 x3 *)
      < T1: SENTENCEVAR{SENTENCEVARrec1}
        T2: EMPTYVAR{EMPTYVARrec1} >

      < T1: SENTENCEVAR{SENTENCEVARrec1}
        T2: EMPTYVAR{EMPTYVARrec1} >

      COMP
         <
          C1: synEMPTY_OPENGERUND IN VERBrec1.synvps AND
              SENTENCEVARrec1 = { senttype   : subordinateclause
                                  PROsubject : true
                                  finiteness : infinite
                                  infsort    : openIng }
          A1: VERBPPROPrec2 := VERBPPROPrec1;
              VERBPPROPrec2.synvpefs := [synEMPTY_OPENGERUND];
              rel1 := indobjrel;
              rel2 := complrel
         >
      DECOMP
         <
          C1: synEMPTY_OPENGERUND IN VERBPPROPrec2.synvpefs AND
              synEMPTY_OPENGERUND IN VERBrec1.synvps AND
              SENTENCEVARrec1 = { senttype   : subordinateclause
                                  PROsubject : true
                                  finiteness : infinite
                                  infsort    : openIng } AND
              rel1 = indobjrel AND
              rel2 = complrel 
          A1: VERBPPROPrec1 := VERBPPROPrec2;
              VERBPPROPrec1.synvpefs := []
         >

>
&

\end{verbatim}
\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] TVerbpattern4
\item[* relevant examples:] In general: IONP\_DO..\\
\begin{description}
\item[subrule 1a/d] IONP\_DONP/CN\\
  (vp123): x1 give x2 x3: I gave him the book.\\
  (vp123): x1 tell x2 x3: I told him a lie (cf.\ the idiom {\em to tell a lie
  \/}: liegen).\\
  (vp123): x1 charge x2 x3: How much do you charge me (for that)?\\
  For a remark about `clitic' DONPs, see below under {\em problems\/}.
\item[subrule 2a/b] EMPTY\_DONP/CN\\
  (vp123): ?x1 refuse EMPTY x3: She refused his offer (IF this exists!).\\
  Note that sentences like {\em I gave a book\/} can be derived in two ways: 
  either from `I gave EMPTY a book' or from `I gave a book (to EMPTY)'. To 
prevent unnecessary ambiguities, it is assumed that all verbs having a version 
with a preposition use this construction as the basic one, and only have the 
IONP\_DONP as an extra possibility. If the IONP is missing, the synpattern will 
therefore be classified as DONP\_EMPTY. This pattern is dealt with in the rule 
TVerbpattern5. Verbs that do not take a construction with a preposition and 
still lack an indirect object should be classified as EMPTY\_DONP. 
Quirk gives the following examples 
of verbs without a prep-construction (p.\ 846): allow (=`give') sb sth, refuse 
sb sth, 
wish sb sth (plus two measure-phrase verbs: charge sb sth, fine sb sth). It is 
quite conceivable that these verbs {\em cannot\/} have an EMPTY as indirect 
object, making {\em refuse sth\/}  different from {\em refuse sb sth\/} 
(cf.\ their translations being {\em iets afslaan\/} and {\em iemand iets 
weigeren\/}. However, also note {\em He refused\/}, which has a PROSENT as the 
only VP-argument, just like {\em He refused to do it\/}, which only takes an 
OPENTOSENT as argument. This will then be the third form of {\em refuse\/}, 
translationg in {\em iets weigeren\/}.\\
Note that when the direct object is not just an NP, but a longer constituent 
(e.g.\ a THATSENT or an OPENTOSENT), the construction with a preposition +
indirect object is less likely. Therefore, in those cases an EMPTY\_DO.. is 
still accepted (see e.g.\ subrules 4, 8, 11). 
\item[subrule 3a/b] IONP\_THATSENT\\
  (vp123): x1 tell x2 x3: He told me that he would be back early.
\item[subrule 4] EMPTY\_THATSENT\\
  (vp123): x1 promise EMPTY x3: We promised that we would be home early.
\item[subrule 5a/b] IONP\_SOPROSENT\\
  (vp123): x1 tell x2 x3: I told him so.
\item[subrule 6a/b] IONP\_QSENT\\
  (vp123): x1 tell x2 x3: Tell me where to go; I asked him if he wanted tea.
\item[subrule 7a/b] IONP\_OPENTOSENT\\
  (vp123): x1 promise x2 x3: John promised Bill to see a doctor\\
  (vp123): x1 persuade x2 x3: John persuaded Bill to see a doctor.\\
 Note that this verbpattern is for both subject and object controllers.
\item[subrule 8] EMPTY\_OPENTOSENT\\
  (vp123): x1 promise x2 x3: I promised EMPTY to come.
\item[subrule 9a/b] IONP\_MEASUREPHRASE\\
  (vp123): x1 cost x2 x3: This cost me \$6; It cost him his head (?!)\\
  (vp123): x1 charge x2 x3: How much do you charge me for that?\\
 Note that at present no restrictions on the kind of OPENNPPROP that can 
function as a 
 measure phrase exist, nor on the verbs that may take a measure phrase. Perhaps 
a verb like {\em cost\/} should have two patterns: the literal reading taking a 
measure phrase, the figurative just any NP (not an NPPROP).
\item[subrule 10] EMPTY\_MEASUREPHRASE\\
  (vp123): x1 cost x2 x3: This costs EMPTY \$6. \\
Note that verbs taking a measure phrase usually do not have a paraphrase with a 
preposition (cf.\ the remarks made under subrule 2), thus allowing a pattern 
with EMPTY\_`DO..'. In this respect measure phrases may prove to be crucially 
different from other verb arguments, which do not seem to allow an EMPTY\_DONP.
\item[subrule 11] EMPTY\_QSENT\\
  (vp123): x1 ask x2 x3: I asked EMPTY why he wanted to leave; - if he wanted 
tea.
\item[subrule 12a/b] IONP\_PROSENT\\
  (vp123): x1 tell x2 x3: I told him.
\item[subrule 13] EMPTY\_PROSENT\\
  (vp123): x1 ask x2 x3: I'll ask.
\item[subrule 14] EMPTY\_OPENGERUND\\
  (vp123): x1 allow x2 x3: I don't allow smoking here.
\end{description}
\item[* language:]  English
\item[* kind of rule:] M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] Verbppropformation
\item[* class:] TC:Verbpatternrules
\item[* date of creation:] 16-DEC-1986 16:43:43, 27-NOV-1987
\item[* author:] Agnes Mijnhout, margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:]
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] Checks whether the category of the variables are compatible with 
the category of the arguments that the verb expects. Note that the order of the 
variables is reversed in the output S-tree.
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals) The arguments 
are introduced, but do {\em not\/} receive case yet (cf.\ Dutch, where the 
indirect object already receives its case). Only after the passive rules will 
it be clear which arguments are still in the VP and thus should receive the 
value [accusative] for their case.\\
Note that a difference between direct and indirect objects is indicated by the 
name the relation bears. It was suggested by Jan Odijk to distinguish only a 
{\em first\/} and a {\em second\/} object in English. However, it is difficult 
to keep track of the implications of such a decision, and it has not been 
implemented.
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:] Each subrule 
handles a specific verbpattern.
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of 
parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:]
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:] none
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item dictionary:\\ Values for the attribute "synvps" in the dictionary for 
BVERBs.
\item rules:\\ These transformations follow the startrules.

\end{enumerate}
