\item[* relevant literature/documents:] doc. nr. 108 (Verbpatterns in Rosetta3)
\item[\ \ remarks:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item problems:\\
If the final NP in subrule 1 (IONP\_DONP: he gave his father a book) is short (
esp.\ the pronominal {\em it\/}), the sentence becomes unacceptable: $^{*}$He 
gave all his friends it. Preposing does not solve this: $^{*}$He gave it all his 
friends. The solution is to 
have a filter following the substitution rules, disallowing the clitic {\em it
\/} after an indirect object. The version with a preposition, available for 
most verbs (see the remarks made under subrule 2), will then be the only 
translation: He gave it {\em to\/} all his friends. For the few verbs not 
having the preposition construction, no simple translation can be found: 
Hij stond het mij toe $\rightarrow$ $^{*}$ He allowed me it, $^{*}$He allowed 
it me, 
$^{*}$He allowed me (from the verb {\em allow sb to do sth\/}, subrule 7).
\item modifications:\\

\end{enumerate}
